<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  =============================================================================
  services/transaction-service/pom.xml
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Purpose:
    Maven module descriptor for the LEDGER bounded context of the CardDemo
    mainframe-to-microservices migration. It joins this module to the
    com.carddemo:carddemo-services reactor, declares the module's dependency
    set, and activates Spring Boot repackaging.

    It carries the migration of four CICS transactions, whose COBOL sources are
    the behavioural oracle and are never modified:
      CT00  transaction list    app/cbl/COTRN00C.cbl  (699 lines)
      CT01  transaction view    app/cbl/COTRN01C.cbl
      CT02  transaction add     app/cbl/COTRN02C.cbl  (783 lines)
      CB00  bill payment        app/cbl/COBIL00C.cbl  (572 lines)

    The module owns the PostgreSQL schema `ledger` and its four tables:
    transactions, daily_transactions, transaction_rejects and
    transaction_category_balances. Their columns derive from the record layouts
    in app/cpy/CVTRA05Y.cpy (TRAN-RECORD, RECLN 350) and app/cpy/CVTRA01Y.cpy
    (TRAN-CAT-BAL-RECORD, 50 bytes, group-level composite key TRAN-CAT-KEY).

    This module is a DEPLOYABLE Spring Boot application, not a library. That
    single fact drives the plugin decision at the foot of this file.
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  WHY (non-obvious design decisions):

    Assumptions: XML 1.0 section 2.5 forbids the two-character sequence of two
      consecutive hyphens anywhere inside a comment. Verified empirically with
      a conforming parser: a hyphen run, a hyphen-pair em-dash, an arrow whose
      head closes the comment early, and a hyphenated line range such as
      L281 then L371 written with a hyphen pair, are ALL rejected as
      not-well-formed, while runs of `=` and `~` parse cleanly. Consequently
      this file deviates from the house mid-rule character used at
      .github/workflows/tests.yml L3, substituting `~` for it, and writes every
      line range in the form "L281 to L371". The deviation is recorded here
      rather than left silent, because an unexplained inconsistency with the
      house geometry would read as carelessness instead of as a constraint.

    Assumptions: the four canonical justification labels below are written in
      the plural, hyphenated form required by the project's Explainability
      rule. The singular variants that appear in .github/workflows/tests.yml
      L19 and L23 and in .gitignore L59 are house exemplars that predate the
      rule; the rule is the binding authority, so the plural form is used
      uniformly and the two forms are never mixed inside this file.

    Alternatives Considered: this module activates spring-boot-maven-plugin
      while services/common-lib/pom.xml deliberately omits it. See the plugin
      declaration for the full reasoning; the short form is that `repackage`
      relocates classes under BOOT-INF/classes, which suits a deployable and
      breaks a library.

    Assumptions: every version this module needs is supplied by the parent, so
      the only literal version element in this file is inside `parent`. The one
      exception is a `project.version` property reference on the reactor
      sibling, justified at its declaration.

    Assumptions: four Spring Boot starters are re-declared here even though
      common-lib already compiles against them, because common-lib marks them
      optional and optional dependencies are not transitive. Omitting them
      yields a NoClassDefFoundError at run time rather than a build failure.

    Alternatives Considered: Lombok, MapStruct, a resilience library, a circuit
      breaker, a cache tier and any sibling-service dependency are all absent
      by decision, not by oversight. Each absence is justified in the block
      that closes the dependency list.

    Trade-offs: this module declares its own starters rather than inheriting a
      wide compile classpath from common-lib. The cost is a longer dependency
      list in each of the eight service POMs; the benefit is that a library
      consumed by eight modules cannot silently place a servlet container on a
      consumer that has no web tier.
  =============================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <!--
    Assumptions: the parent aggregator supplies the Java release level, the
    resource encoding, the Checkstyle documentation gate bound to `validate`,
    the Surefire and Failsafe executions, and every dependency version this
    module consumes. `relativePath` points at the reactor parent on disk so a
    module build resolves it without first installing it to the local
    repository. This is the ONLY place a literal version element appears in
    this file: a hard-coded version on any dependency or plugin below would
    desynchronise from the reactor the moment the parent version is bumped,
    and Maven would not report the drift because both values would still
    resolve.
  -->
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
    <artifactId>carddemo-services</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>transaction-service</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>CardDemo Transaction Service</name>
  <description>
    Ledger bounded context of the CardDemo migration. Owns the `ledger`
    PostgreSQL schema (transactions, daily_transactions, transaction_rejects,
    transaction_category_balances) and migrates CT00 transaction list
    (COTRN00C), CT01 transaction view (COTRN01C), CT02 transaction add
    (COTRN02C) and CB00 bill payment (COBIL00C) from CICS and VSAM to a
    stateless REST service on Spring Boot.
  </description>

  <dependencies>

    <!--
      Assumptions: `project.version` is a property reference, not a literal, and
      it resolves from the version inherited from `parent` above, so the reactor
      sibling is always pinned to the same version as the module consuming it
      and no version string is duplicated into this file. A hard-coded version
      here would silently desynchronise from the reactor on the next parent
      version bump.

      Alternatives Considered: omitting the version entirely and relying on the
      parent's dependencyManagement to supply it. Rejected because that is
      correct only while such an entry exists: verified with a reactor build,
      a sibling declaration with no version and no managing entry fails with
      "'dependencies.dependency.version' for com.carddemo:common-lib:jar is
      missing" and Maven then reports "The build could not read 1 project", so
      the module becomes unbuildable rather than merely mis-versioned. The
      property reference is correct whether or not the parent manages the
      entry, because an explicit version and a managed version resolve to the
      identical reactor value.

      This is the ONLY intra-reactor dependency permitted for this module. It
      supplies the shared kernel: the keyset page envelope that replaces the
      COMMAREA browse cursor of app/cbl/COTRN00C.cbl L281 to L371, the date
      edit validator standing in for the CALL to CSUTLDTC at
      app/cbl/COTRN02C.cbl L393 and L413, the fixed-point money type, the
      fixed-width and zoned-decimal codecs, and the shared error model.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: services/common-lib/pom.xml marks the four starters below
      `optional`, and Maven does not propagate optional dependencies to
      consumers, so none of them arrives transitively from the shared kernel.
      common-lib does that deliberately: a plain compile-scope declaration
      there would place a servlet container and a full security filter chain on
      every one of the eight consuming modules, including any with no web tier,
      which would dissolve the boundary the parent POM draws on purpose. The
      accepted trade-off is that each consumer restates what it actually uses.

      Trade-offs: omitting any one of these four does NOT break the build. It
      breaks at run time, as a NoClassDefFoundError raised on the first request
      that touches the missing tier, which is why the omission is easy to make
      and expensive to diagnose. That asymmetry is the reason all four are
      listed explicitly here instead of being left to inheritance.

      Each is load-bearing for this bounded context: `web` serves the four
      migrated transactions as REST endpoints; `validation` carries the field
      edit chain transcribed from app/cbl/COTRN02C.cbl; `security` plus
      `oauth2-resource-server` replace the client-echoed CDEMO-USER-TYPE field
      of app/cpy/COCOM01Y.cpy L19 to L44 with a signed token claim, so the
      client can no longer assert its own authority.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the four `ledger` entities map the fixed-width VSAM records
      one field at a time, and the three keyset access paths replace the CICS
      browse verbs of app/cbl/COTRN00C.cbl (STARTBR at L281, READNEXT at L286,
      READPREV at L340, ENDBR at L322). The balance-affecting rewrite at
      app/cbl/COBIL00C.cbl L379 sits inside one CICS syncpoint, so it needs a
      single declarative transaction boundary rather than two independent
      writes; JPA supplies that, and it supplies the optimistic version column
      that replaces the before-image comparison the COBOL performs by hand
      across the pseudo-conversational gap.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: two independent consumers poll the health endpoint this
      starter publishes, and both are defined outside this module: the load
      balancer target group that decides whether to route to a task, and the
      HEALTHCHECK instruction in this module's own Dockerfile. Dropping the
      starter would leave both probing a 404 and the container would be
      reported unhealthy while the application itself was serving correctly.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: Spring Boot 4 moved autoconfiguration out of the single
      spring-boot-autoconfigure artifact into per-technology modules, so
      FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in
      org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-flyway, which this starter contributes
      along with spring-boot-jdbc. The two org.flywaydb artifacts below supply
      the migration engine and the PostgreSQL dialect, but neither activates it.
      Verified empirically against this exact dependency set: with the starter
      absent, spring-boot-autoconfigure carries zero Flyway classes, no
      FlywayAutoConfiguration is registered, every spring.flyway.* key in
      src/main/resources/application.yml is inert, and the application starts and
      reports healthy against a database on which no migration has ever run. On a
      schema that is the system of record for posted money that failure is silent
      and total, which is why the starter is declared explicitly rather than
      assumed to arrive with the engine. No version appears here; the Spring Boot
      parent manages this artifact.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-flyway</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: flyway-core is no longer declared directly. The
      starter above brings it transitively at the version the aggregator's
      flyway.version property pins, so a direct declaration would add a second
      place that version could be edited while contributing nothing to the
      classpath. The seven other schema owners in this reactor declare the same
      pair, so the shape is uniform rather than per module.

      Assumptions: flyway-database-postgresql stays declared in its own right and
      is NOT redundant beside the starter. Flyway 10 moved database-specific
      support out of the core artifact into per-database modules, and that split
      persists at the 13.0.0 the parent manages, so core on its own does not fail
      the build: it fails when the service first opens a connection and tries to
      resolve the PostgreSQL database type, because nothing on the classpath
      claims the jdbc:postgresql URL. Neither artifact is re-pinned here; the
      parent overrides the Spring Boot default of 12.4.0 up to 13.0.0, and
      restating a version in this file would fork that decision.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the browser client is generated against the OpenAPI 3.1
      contract this starter serves from
      src/main/resources/openapi/transaction-api.yaml, so the contract is a
      published interface of this module and not developer convenience. Version
      3.0.3 is managed by the parent, since Spring Boot does not manage
      springdoc.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: HikariCP and Flyway both instantiate the driver reflectively
      from the JDBC URL, so no class in this module names a PostgreSQL type at
      compile time. Runtime scope keeps the driver out of the compile classpath,
      which prevents a repository or mapper from accidentally compiling against
      a vendor-specific type and hard-wiring this bounded context to one engine.
      Version 42.7.13 comes from the parent, which overrides the Spring Boot
      default of 42.7.11.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: Spring Boot discovers this registry through
      auto-configuration by its presence on the classpath; no application class
      imports it. Runtime scope is therefore sufficient, and compile scope would
      widen the compile classpath for a dependency nothing references. The
      version resolves to 1.17.0 through the micrometer BOM that Spring Boot
      4.1.0 imports transitively.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the layering rules this module is held to are authored once,
      at
      services/common-lib/src/test/java/com/carddemo/common/architecture/LayeringRulesTest.java.
      The engine below is the assertion API those rules are written against, and
      it is test scoped so it can never reach the production classpath of the
      module it polices. Version 1.4.2 is managed by the parent, since Spring
      Boot does not manage ArchUnit.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Refactoring Rationale: the comment above this pair previously recorded that
      the rules could NOT be delivered as a dependency, because common-lib bound
      no test-jar goal, and concluded that the engine alone would let this module
      "run its own copy of the rules". There was no copy to run: the engine
      supplies an assertion API and no rules, and Maven resolution passes a
      module's main classes to its consumers and never its test classes, so
      nothing ever analysed com.carddemo.transaction. common-lib now binds that
      goal, before its `package` phase so a plain `mvn test` sees a real file, and
      this declaration consumes the artifact it produces. The architecture-rules
      Surefire execution in services/pom.xml scans it into this module's own test
      run, which is what puts this module's compiled classes in front of the
      rules.

      Assumptions: the artifact carries the architecture package alone, so
      nothing else from the shared kernel's test tree arrives with it, and its
      version and scope come from the aggregator's managed set.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.carddemo</groupId>
      <artifactId>common-lib</artifactId>
      <type>test-jar</type>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      Assumptions: the artifact identifiers below carry the `testcontainers-`
      prefix because Testcontainers 2.x renamed every module, and the parent
      imports the 2.0.5 BOM that Spring Boot 4.1.0 already pins. Verified with a
      reactor build: the 1.x identifiers `junit-jupiter` and `postgresql` are
      absent from that BOM, so declaring them versionless fails with
      "'dependencies.dependency.version' for org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter:jar
      is missing" and the POM becomes unreadable rather than merely stale. The
      prefixed identifiers resolve to 2.0.5 at test scope with no version here.

      Alternatives Considered: an in-memory database for the repository suite.
      Rejected because the behaviour under test is engine-specific: the three
      keyset access paths and the deliberately NON-UNIQUE index over the
      processing timestamp, which stands in for the batch alternate index over
      app/cpy/CVTRA05Y.cpy field TRAN-PROC-TS at zero-based offset 304, cannot
      be exercised on H2. The RepositoryIT suite Failsafe is bound to run exists
      precisely to prove those paths, so it must run against real PostgreSQL or it
      proves nothing. That suite is authored at a later index of the same plan;
      this dependency and that binding are what let it run against a real engine
      on its first execution rather than being retrofitted afterwards.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers-postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!--
      DELIBERATE EXCLUSIONS. Each entry below is an absence with a reasonable
      alternative, recorded so that a reader does not restore it as an apparent
      omission.

      Alternatives Considered: Lombok, for generated accessors and builders.
      Rejected because the generated members carry no Javadoc, so the very
      accessors it produces would fail the Javadoc gate the parent binds at
      `validate`; the tool would breach the documentation rule it was added to
      save effort under. Java 21 record types with explicit constructors give
      the same brevity while leaving every member documentable.

      Alternatives Considered: MapStruct, for copybook-to-DTO mapping. Rejected
      for two independent reasons. Its newest published release is a beta. More
      decisively, the mapping in this module is not mechanical: it drops FILLER
      (app/cpy/CVTRA05Y.cpy L18 and app/cpy/CVTRA01Y.cpy L10), masks the
      primary account number to its last four digits, and carries money as a
      string so no client parses it into a binary floating-point double. Each of
      those needs a justification at the mapping site, and a generated mapper
      has nowhere to put one.

      Alternatives Considered: io.github.resilience4j:resilience4j-spring-boot3,
      or org.springframework.retry:spring-retry, for retry and rate limiting.
      Both rejected: the resilience4j artifact targets Spring Boot 3.x, and
      spring-retry is superseded. Spring Framework 7.0.8, which arrives inside
      the Spring Boot 4.1.0 parent, relocated the capability into the core
      container as org.springframework.resilience.annotation.Retryable and
      ConcurrencyLimit, enabled by EnableResilientMethods. Verified against the
      shipped jar: the retry attribute is `maxRetries`, so total attempts equal
      one plus that value, and the older EnableRetry annotation is not present
      in the artifact at all. Adding either library would shadow a capability
      already on the classpath.

      Alternatives Considered: a circuit breaker. Rejected because the only
      synchronous hops out of this module stay inside the private network behind
      an internal load balancer under bounded connect and read timeouts. A
      breaker would introduce an open-circuit state, and therefore a new failure
      mode, without removing the failure mode it exists to contain.

      Alternatives Considered: a Maven dependency on batch-service, which posts
      to the same tables. Rejected on two grounds. batch-service and this module
      are two views of one physical `ledger` schema and agree through column
      names, types and decimal scale under a narrowly scoped cross-schema grant,
      never through shared code. A code dependency would also breach the
      ArchUnit rule that forbids importing another service's domain package, so
      the layering test would fail the build. common-lib is the only permitted
      intra-reactor dependency.

      Assumptions: spring-boot-starter-batch, io.awspring.cloud and
      software.amazon.awssdk are all absent because this bounded context owns no
      queue and no job repository. It therefore configures no queue client and
      no batch infrastructure, and the jobs that sweep `ledger` tables live in
      batch-service. A Spring Batch starter here would create job repository
      tables this schema does not own.

      Alternatives Considered: a Redis or in-process cache tier. Rejected
      because the VSAM baseline has no cache and adding one would introduce a
      staleness window the golden-master comparison would correctly flag as a
      parity failure. Kafka and Kinesis are likewise absent: the messaging this
      migration needs is request and reply, not a stream, and this context
      publishes neither.
    -->
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>

      <!--
        Alternatives Considered: leaving this plugin undeclared and inheriting
        only the parent's plugin management, exactly as
        services/common-lib/pom.xml does. That is right for common-lib and wrong
        here, and the asymmetry between the two POMs is deliberate rather than an
        inconsistency.

        The `repackage` goal rewrites the module artifact into an executable
        archive whose application classes move from the archive root down to
        BOOT-INF/classes, with dependencies nested under BOOT-INF/lib and a
        launcher installed as the main class. A library resolved onto the
        compile classpath of eight sibling modules must stay a plain archive,
        because a consumer's compiler does not look under BOOT-INF/classes and
        would see none of its types; that is why common-lib omits the plugin.

        This module is the opposite case. It is a deployable whose container
        runtime stage launches a single self-contained archive, so it MUST
        activate the plugin to produce one. Without this note the two POMs look
        arbitrarily different, and a future reader would be tempted to make them
        match and break one of the two.

        No version is declared: the Spring Boot parent's plugin management pins
        it in lockstep with the framework version, and verified against a real
        reactor build it resolves cleanly with no version stated here.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED HERE. As with the dependency exclusions, each
        absence below is a decision with a plausible alternative.

        Assumptions: the Checkstyle plugin is bound by the parent to the
        `validate` phase, so the documentation gate fires on every local build
        rather than only in continuous integration. The parent supplies engine
        13.8.0, a configuration location of config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml,
        violation severity of warning, console output, and failure on violation.
        Re-declaring the plugin in this module would silently override whichever
        of those settings the redeclaration omitted, and weakening the gate is a
        breach of the project's Explainability rule rather than a build
        preference. In particular a suppressions location must never be set
        here: the suppression file is wired from inside checkstyle.xml by a
        filter module declared non-optional, so the configuration is fail-closed
        and a module-local override would reopen it. No module-local Checkstyle
        or suppressions file is added for the same reason.

        Alternatives Considered: enforcing the layering invariant a second time
        through the Checkstyle import-control module. Rejected because two
        enforcement points for one invariant drift apart as soon as one is
        edited, and the invariant is already expressed as an ArchUnit test, so
        it executes on every build and cannot quietly rot the way a static
        allow-list can.

        Assumptions: Surefire and Failsafe are configured by the parent, which
        runs Test-suffixed classes at the `test` phase and the
        RepositoryIT-suffixed integration classes at `integration-test` and
        `verify`. Continuous integration collects reports from the DEFAULT
        output directories under this module's target directory, so relocating
        or renaming either report directory here would leave the pipeline
        collecting nothing while every test still passed locally. Neither plugin
        is re-declared, and neither report directory is moved.

        Assumptions: no coverage, formatter, shade, release or site plugin is
        declared, and no compiler plugin is re-declared. The parent already sets
        the compiler release level, and a shade plugin in particular would
        conflict with the Spring Boot repackaging above by rewriting the same
        artifact. No repository or plugin-repository element is declared either:
        every coordinate in this file resolves from Maven Central, and this
        project has no private registry to point at.

        Assumptions: no properties block is declared. The parent owns the Java
        release level and the source and resource encodings, and restating any
        of them here would create a second place to change them.
      -->
    </plugins>
  </build>

</project>
